    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. To help my client visualize it, could you expand on the cellular level? Like, how does the cell actually \"sense\" that leucine is present, and are there any specific metabolic stressors that might disrupt that mTOR signaling? I want to make sure they understand why timing and quality of protein really matter.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The cell senses leucine through the Rag GTPase complex located in the lysosomal membrane, which acts as the central input hub for amino acid signals. Upon detecting high leucine concentrations, GATOR1 is inhibited, allowing GATOR2 to activate the Rag GTPases and translocate mTORC1 to the lysosome where it becomes fully active. However, this process can be disrupted by metabolic stressors such as high-serine levels, chronic inflammation, or excessive calorie restriction, all of which can lead to GATOR1 reactivation and mTORC1 inhibition. Consequently, the specific quality of protein ensures leucine exceeds the threshold needed to bypass inhibition, while precise timing optimizes nutrient availability exactly when mTOR signaling is most sensitive to drive acute protein synthesis.",
      "source": "target",
